WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:20.123
<v Host>Welcome to Japanese Daily News. Today is May twenty-sixth. Today's story is big: the Japanese government wants to cut the sales tax on food all the way down to zero. This affects everyone who eats in Japan — so, everyone. Let's hear the news first, then break it down.

00:00:20.724 --> 00:00:29.388
<v News>日本政府は、食料品の消費税をゼロパーセントにする方針を発表しました。

00:00:30.088 --> 00:00:45.856
<v News>今、日本の消費税は10パーセントです。食料品は8パーセントの軽減税率が適用されています。これをゼロにする計画です。

00:00:46.555 --> 00:01:02.179
<v News>高市早苗首相は、「物価が高くなって、国民の生活が苦しくなっている。食べ物の税金をなくすことで、家計を助けたい」と話しました。

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:17.447
<v News>この減税は二年間の期間限定です。まず、国会で法案を提出して、早ければ二千二十六年度中に始まる予定です。

00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:31.828
<v News>例えば、今まで百八円だったおにぎりが、百円になります。千八十円だった食料品の買い物が、千円になります。

00:01:32.528 --> 00:01:44.287
<v News>ただし、お酒と外食は対象外です。スーパーやコンビニで買う食べ物と飲み物だけが対象になります。

00:01:44.987 --> 00:01:54.323
<v News>野党からは、「ゼロではなく、すべての消費税を下げるべきだ」という意見も出ています。

00:01:55.024 --> 00:01:59.536
<v Host>All right. That was a lot. Let's go through it step by step.

00:02:00.135 --> 00:02:01.719
<v Host>The opening line:

00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:12.975
<v Slow>日本政府は、食料品の消費税をゼロパーセントにする方針を発表しました。

00:02:13.776 --> 00:02:20.951
<v Host>The Japanese government announced a plan to make the sales tax on food zero percent. Three key words here.

00:02:21.551 --> 00:02:24.288
<v Vocab>食料品

00:02:24.687 --> 00:02:34.792
<v Host>Food products, or groceries. This word covers rice, vegetables, snacks — anything you eat. You'll see it on signs in Japanese supermarkets.

00:02:35.391 --> 00:02:37.936
<v Vocab>消費税

00:02:38.336 --> 00:02:45.584
<v Host>Consumption tax. Japan calls its sales tax this way. Right now it's ten percent on most things.

00:02:46.183 --> 00:02:48.512
<v Vocab>方針

00:02:48.912 --> 00:02:56.903
<v Host>Policy or plan. When the government uses this word, it means they've decided on a direction but haven't passed a law yet.

00:02:57.503 --> 00:03:18.864
<v Host>Now, some background. Japan's sales tax used to be just three percent when it started in nineteen eighty-nine. It went up to five percent, then eight, and finally ten percent in twenty nineteen. Food stayed at eight — that's called a reduced rate. Now the plan is to take food all the way to zero. Next sentence:

00:03:19.463 --> 00:03:28.223
<v Slow>食料品は8パーセントの軽減税率が適用されています。

00:03:29.024 --> 00:03:33.536
<v Host>A reduced tax rate of eight percent is being applied to food products.

00:03:34.135 --> 00:03:37.207
<v Vocab>軽減税率

00:03:37.608 --> 00:03:42.743
<v Host>Reduced tax rate. This is a compound word. Let's break it apart.

00:03:43.343 --> 00:03:45.623
<v Vocab>軽減

00:03:46.024 --> 00:03:49.431
<v Host>It means reduction, or making something lighter.

00:03:50.032 --> 00:03:52.479
<v Vocab>税率

00:03:52.879 --> 00:04:01.039
<v Host>It means tax rate. So together: a lightened tax rate. You'll hear this word a lot in Japanese news about economics.

00:04:01.639 --> 00:04:18.800
<v Host>Notice the grammar here: the sentence uses the passive form plus the ongoing state. It's not 'someone applies the rate.' It's 'the rate is being applied.' This pattern is very common in news Japanese when describing rules or policies that are currently in effect.

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:23.000
<v Host>Now, the Prime Minister explained why this matters:

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:31.495
<v Slow>物価が高くなって、国民の生活が苦しくなっている。

00:04:32.295 --> 00:04:36.903
<v Host>Prices have gone up, and people's daily lives have become difficult.

00:04:37.504 --> 00:04:39.663
<v Vocab>物価

00:04:40.064 --> 00:04:51.031
<v Host>Price level — not the price of one item, but prices in general. When Japanese people say 'the cost of living is high,' they often use this word. For example:

00:04:51.632 --> 00:04:56.000
<v Slow>東京は物価が高いです。

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.648
<v Host>Tokyo has a high cost of living. Very useful sentence to know.

00:05:02.247 --> 00:05:04.648
<v Vocab>苦しい

00:05:05.048 --> 00:05:13.975
<v Host>Tough, painful, or difficult. It's used for physical pain, but also financial hardship. You might hear people say:

00:05:14.576 --> 00:05:18.247
<v Slow>生活が苦しい。

00:05:19.048 --> 00:05:35.319
<v Host>Life is tough. A common expression. Why is the cost of living going up? The main reason is the weak yen. Japan imports a lot of food, and when the yen drops, imports get more expensive. That's why the government feels the need to act.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.776
<v Host>The news gives a very concrete example:

00:05:39.375 --> 00:05:46.696
<v Slow>今まで百八円だったおにぎりが、百円になります。

00:05:47.495 --> 00:05:59.423
<v Host>An onigiri that cost one hundred and eight yen will become one hundred yen. Eight yen doesn't sound like much, but multiply that across every grocery trip, and it adds up.

00:06:00.024 --> 00:06:02.327
<v Vocab>今まで

00:06:02.728 --> 00:06:10.983
<v Host>Until now. A time expression you'll use constantly in Japanese. It marks the before in a before-and-after comparison.

00:06:11.584 --> 00:06:13.192
<v Host>But there's a catch:

00:06:13.791 --> 00:06:19.983
<v Slow>ただし、お酒と外食は対象外です。

00:06:20.783 --> 00:06:24.312
<v Host>However, alcohol and eating out are excluded.

00:06:24.911 --> 00:06:27.216
<v Vocab>ただし

00:06:27.615 --> 00:06:34.863
<v Host>However. This is the formal version. In casual speech you'd say 'demo' or 'dakedo.'

00:06:35.463 --> 00:06:37.672
<v Vocab>だけど

00:06:38.072 --> 00:06:45.175
<v Host>But in news and official documents, you'll always hear 'tadashi.' Very clean, very precise.

00:06:45.776 --> 00:06:48.367
<v Vocab>対象外

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:51.192
<v Host>Outside the scope, excluded.

00:06:51.791 --> 00:06:54.144
<v Vocab>対象

00:06:54.543 --> 00:07:07.887
<v Host>The opposite — without the 'gai' — means 'target' or 'covered.' So if you buy beer at a convenience store — still taxed. If you eat ramen at a restaurant — still taxed. Only groceries you take home.

00:07:08.487 --> 00:07:11.103
<v Host>And the opposition had something to say:

00:07:11.704 --> 00:07:20.728
<v Slow>野党からは、すべての消費税を下げるべきだという意見も出ています。

00:07:21.528 --> 00:07:29.255
<v Host>The opposition parties say they should lower all consumption tax, not just on food. The grammar point here is 'beki da.'

00:07:29.855 --> 00:07:32.016
<v Vocab>べきだ

00:07:32.415 --> 00:07:40.648
<v Host>— 'should do.' It expresses a strong opinion. When politicians use it, they're making an argument, not a suggestion.

00:07:41.247 --> 00:07:47.295
<v Host>Now let's listen to the full story one more time. See how much more you understand now.

00:07:47.896 --> 00:07:56.560
<v News>日本政府は、食料品の消費税をゼロパーセントにする方針を発表しました。

00:07:57.259 --> 00:08:13.028
<v News>今、日本の消費税は10パーセントです。食料品は8パーセントの軽減税率が適用されています。これをゼロにする計画です。

00:08:13.728 --> 00:08:29.351
<v News>高市早苗首相は、「物価が高くなって、国民の生活が苦しくなっている。食べ物の税金をなくすことで、家計を助けたい」と話しました。

00:08:30.052 --> 00:08:44.620
<v News>この減税は二年間の期間限定です。まず、国会で法案を提出して、早ければ二千二十六年度中に始まる予定です。

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:59.000
<v News>例えば、今まで百八円だったおにぎりが、百円になります。千八十円だった食料品の買い物が、千円になります。

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:11.460
<v News>ただし、お酒と外食は対象外です。スーパーやコンビニで買う食べ物と飲み物だけが対象になります。

00:09:12.159 --> 00:09:21.495
<v News>野党からは、「ゼロではなく、すべての消費税を下げるべきだ」という意見も出ています。

00:09:22.196 --> 00:09:28.460
<v Host>Now a quick quiz. I'll ask three questions in Japanese. Try to answer before I do.

00:09:29.059 --> 00:09:36.283
<v Slow>今、食料品の消費税は何パーセントですか？

00:09:37.083 --> 00:09:44.067
<v Host>What is the current sales tax on food? The answer: eight percent. The plan is to take it to zero.

00:09:44.668 --> 00:09:48.532
<v Slow>お酒は対象ですか？

00:09:49.331 --> 00:09:53.364
<v Host>Is alcohol included? No. It's excluded.

00:09:53.964 --> 00:09:58.692
<v Slow>この減税は何年間ですか？

00:09:59.491 --> 00:10:05.395
<v Host>How many years will this tax cut last? Two years. It's a limited-time measure.

00:10:05.995 --> 00:10:21.884
<v Host>Let's wrap up with a vocabulary recap. Food products, groceries. Consumption tax. Reduced tax rate. Price level, cost of living. Tough, difficult. However. Excluded, outside the scope.

00:10:22.484 --> 00:10:36.475
<v Host>That's today's Japanese Daily News. Full transcript with furigana at podcast.jpnotes.dev. If you found this helpful, subscribe on Apple Podcasts or Spotify. See you tomorrow.

00:10:37.076 --> 00:10:39.331
<v News>また明日。
